tish author of espionage novels. During the 1950s and the 1960s, he worked for both the Security Service and the Secret Intelligence Service, and began writing novels under his pen name. \", \"source_role\": \"document\", \"source_message_indices\": []}, {\"id\": \"s35\", \"doc_id\": \"hotpotqa:1471\", \"start\": 301, \"end\": 517, \"text\": \"His third novel, \\\"The Spy Who Came in from the Cold\\\" (1963), became an international best-seller and remains one of his best-known works. Following the success of this novel, he left MI6 to become a full-time author.\", \"source_role\": \"document\", \"source_message_indices\": []}, {\"id\": \"s36\", \"doc_id\": \"hotpotqa:8827\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 340, \"text\": \"Anne Perry\\nAnne Perry (born 28 October 1938 as Juliet Marion Hulme) is an English author of historical detective fiction, best known for her Thomas Pitt and William Monk series. At the age of fifteen she was convicted of participating in the murder of her friend's mother, in 1954. She changed her name after serving her five-year sentence.\", \"source_role\": \"document\", \"source_message_indices\": []}, {\"id\": \"s37\", \"doc_id\": \"hotpotqa:1470\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 225, \"text\": \"Call for the Dead\\nCall for the Dead is John le Carré's first novel, published in 1961. It introduces George Smiley, the most famous of le Carré's recurring characters, in a story about East German spies inside Great Britain. \", \"source_role\": \"document\", \"source_message_indices\": []}, {\"id\": \"s38\", \"doc_id\": \"hotpotqa:1470\", \"start\": 225, \"end\": 435, \"text\": \"It also introduces a fictional version of British Intelligence, called \\\"the Circus\\\" because of its location in Cambridge Circus, that is apparently based on MI6 and that recurs throughout le Carré's spy novels.\", \"source_role\": \"document\", \"source_message_indices\": []}]}"
